{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-337260",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=0,\\,\\pi .$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{7}=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{7}.$ But since $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{7}$ has no solution.\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=0,\\,{x}_{2}=\\pi .\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
